  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Scattered afternoon/evening showers/storms. \nSummertime pattern continues.\n\nThe upper ridge of high pressure over the TN valley is forecast\nto weaken and retrograde farther west and become centered over \nthe Ark-La-Tex by the weekend. This will allow a front over the \nGreat Lakes to sag south over the Mid-Atlantic with increasing \nmoisture leading to a daily chance for showers and a few storms \npersisting through next week. Overall, severe threat remains low\ngiven lack of shear to support organized deep convection. \n\nShower/Storm coverage through this evening should be rather\nlimited, with best coverage over higher terrain and into Blue\nRidge foothills where daytime heating and low level convergence\nsupports cloud build-ups and showers. MLCAPE ranges from\n500-1000 j/kg...modest at best, but DCAPE is close to 1000 which\nsuggests healthy downdrafts with gusts from even the weaker\nshowers potentially producing some gusts up to 25 mph. Stronger\ncells may be capable of 30-40 mph gusts. Loss of daytime heating\nshould result in quick demise of activity after sunset. \n \nFriday will be a bit more moist with higher PWATs...the shower \ncoverage more prolific than today yet lacking much in the way \nof organization due to weak shear. However, can't rule out a \nstrong pulse storm or two per summerlike temperatures leading to\nsteep low level lapse rates. \n\nThe weekend looks mostly cloudy with front stalled over the\narea. We may end up on the north side of the front Saturday with\nshallow northeast wind and clouds muting temperatures before\ntrending warmer Sunday and Monday as front waffles back to the \nnorth. Regardless of its position, looking at scattered showers\nthis weekend with opportunity for wetting precipitation \npersisting through the middle of next week as the pattern \nremains stuck.",
